# Expectation record for 12_preprocessor/006_pragma_and_line.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_pragma_and_line
area               = 12_preprocessor
description        = Pragma acceptance observed through properties the standard fixes -- an unrecognised pragma must be ignored, and a diagnostic-control pragma cannot change observable behaviour -- together with the effect of a line directive on the source-position macros, including a virtual file name
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour. There is no signed overflow: every
computed value is a small non-negative int, the difference of two line numbers, or the 0-or-1 result
of a comparison. There is no shift operator, no division and no modulo. No object is read before it is
written -- both int locals are initialised at their points of declaration from the line-number macro
-- no object is modified twice between sequence points, no pointer value is converted to an integer,
and no address is printed. No structure object is ever created: the structure types are named only
inside sizeof and alignof, so no member is accessed and no padding byte is read.

THE ONLY INDEXING IS IN BOUNDS, stated at length because it is the one place a bound could be
stressed. Two subscripts are applied to the file-name macro's string literal: index 0, which every
non-empty string has, and index len - 1, where len is the length this same program has just computed
for that same literal. The literal there is the virtual name a line directive supplied above, so len
is 14 and both subscripts name a character of the literal rather than its terminator or anything past
it. The length helper reads the next character only while the previous one was not the terminator, so
it stops on the terminator and never forms a pointer past the end. Its counter is an int and the only
strings it walks are a handful of characters long, so neither an overflow nor a narrowing conversion
can arise. No aliasing rule is stressed anywhere: there is no cast between object types, no union, and
no pointer arithmetic other than those two subscripts.

NEITHER PRAGMA CAN AFFECT WHAT THE PROGRAM DOES, and that is what makes the two assertions about them
sound. C11 6.10.6p1 requires an implementation to IGNORE a pragma it does not recognise, so the
invented, namespaced pragma between the two identical structure declarations must leave their layout
untouched -- which is exactly what the size and alignment equalities assert. And a diagnostic-control
pragma changes only which messages the translator emits: standard error is captured into finding
artifacts but never compared, so recognising or ignoring it leaves every printed byte identical. Both
assertions therefore hold under every conforming implementation and are broken only by one that acts
on a request it was required to discard.

NO DIAGNOSTIC DIRECTIVE APPEARS ON ANY PATH. The file contains exactly six directives -- four pragmas
and two line directives -- and neither an error directive nor a warning directive is among them, on
any branch, because the file has no conditional compilation at all. An error directive would make
translation fail by design, and a warning directive would write to standard error, which this suite
captures into finding artifacts but never compares; either one would therefore be untestable noise
rather than a comparison. Measured: every one of the twelve cells produced zero bytes on standard
error.

A line directive numbers the line that FOLLOWS it, so every printing call that reads the line-number
macro sits on the line immediately after its own directive: the printed number is the one the
directive set rather than a function of where the rest of this file happens to sit, and the first two
reads are on adjacent lines, so their difference is one wherever they appear. Measured with the
reference compiler: after the directive selecting 700 the macro reads 700 on the next line and 701 on
the line after it, and after the directive selecting 900 it reads 900 on the next line.

DETERMINISM. Ten of the eleven printed values are an int written with the %d specifier. The eleventh
is the only %s in the program, and its argument is the file-name macro AFTER a line directive has
supplied a virtual name, so what it prints is a string constant written in this source rather than a
path the harness chose -- which is the whole reason it is safe to print in full. Before that directive
the same macro is reduced to the relation "is not empty" and its value never reaches stdout. Nothing
target-varying, address-derived, time-derived, random or locale-dependent is printed. No plain char
value is printed: char signedness was measured as signed on x86_64 and i686 and unsigned on aarch64
and riscv64, so a plain-char value would diverge for a reason that is not a defect. No raw type width
is printed: sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) were measured as 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three
targets. No structure size and no alignment is printed as an absolute value. No translation-date and
no translation-time macro is used. Iteration order is fixed by the literal the length helper walks.
main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is truncated.

HERMETICITY. Every input is a literal in this source. Nothing is opened, nothing is read from the
environment, the command line, the clock or the file system, no storage is allocated, and printf is
the only library routine called, so a cell is reproducible from this source and this record alone.

GATE. The default warning gate applies unmodified and no `ub_audit_flags` key appears:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror. Measured clean -- exit status
0 with not one diagnostic -- with all four reference drivers and with the alternate reference compiler,
and clean under -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all, which compiled, ran, exited 0
and printed nothing on standard error. Both gates are reference-compiler-only and are never applied to
the compiler under test, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is untouched.

NO HEADER IS INCLUDED and printf is declared by hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`. The compiler
under test bundles only freestanding headers and ships no standard I/O header, so naming one would
fail on its side of the reference comparison while succeeding on the reference side -- a spurious
divergence caused by the test rather than by a compiler.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
WHY THE PACKING PRAGMA WAS REMOVED, AND WHY NO GATE DEVIATION REPLACED IT.

An earlier form of this program spelled the pragma pack(push, 1) / pack(push, 2) / pack(pop) and
printed the EXACT size, alignment and member offsets of four structures, one per state of that stack.
That subject is unsound for a differential oracle on two counts, either sufficient on its own. The
behaviour of a RECOGNISED pragma is implementation-defined and C11 6.10.6p1 permits it to make
translation fail outright, so a compiler that recognises pack and declines this particular request is
within its rights and would be reported as defective. And no repository document describes a packing
pragma for the compiler under test at all -- the documented inventory names the directive but not one
pragma it honours -- so a divergence arising from the push/pop lifecycle could not be attributed to a
documented limitation either, leaving it neither a sound comparison nor a recordable expected
divergence.

The four structures, their eight layout values and the two member round trips have therefore been
REMOVED FROM THE PROGRAM AND FROM THE GOLDEN RECORD TOGETHER, so that the source, this record and the
expected output describe one and the same portable test. Nothing about packing is dropped from the
corpus: the layout effect of packing is exercised by
08_gcc_extensions/005_attribute_packed_aligned.c, whose subject is the packed and aligned attributes
that the repository's own extension inventory does name as supported, which is a materially stronger
authority than a pragma no document mentions.

What replaced it takes its authority from the standard rather than from any implementation's
documentation: 6.10.6p1's closing requirement that an unrecognised pragma is ignored, asserted through
the size and the alignment of two otherwise identical structure declarations that straddle one.
NEITHER SIZE NOR ALIGNMENT IS PRINTED AS AN ABSOLUTE VALUE -- only the equality of the two -- so no
implementation-defined layout choice can reach the output while the requirement stays observable. Each
structure holds one unsigned int between two unsigned char members, and every layout a conforming
implementation may choose for that, on any of the four targets, satisfies both equalities, because the
two declarations are identical in every respect.

WHAT THE EQUALITIES DO AND DO NOT REPORT, recorded because it decides how a future divergence must be
read, and because it is what stops a maintainer "improving" this program by printing the sizes
themselves. An implementation that simply ignores the unrecognised pragma -- which is what the
standard demands -- passes. The equalities fall to 0 only for an implementation that acted on a
request it was required to discard. An implementation that instead REJECTS the unrecognised pragma
outright does not fail the equalities at all: it fails to translate, and because 6.10.6p1's
requirement to ignore is normative rather than permissive, that compile failure is a genuine finding
rather than an implementation choice. This is also why the record carries no expected-divergence
marker. Both directives in this program are documented as supported by the compiler under test -- its
preprocessor directive handler names pragma and line among the directives it processes, and its source
map tracks line-directive overrides -- so there is no documented limitation available to cite, and a
divergence here must be delivered as a finding with its minimized reproducer instead of being
classified away as expected.

MEASURED, AND WHY THIS RECORD DECLARES NO `ub_audit_flags` DEVIATION. Both reference compilers
diagnose an unknown pragma under the gate's -Wall, through -Werror=unknown-pragmas, and the reference
compiler diagnoses the standard STDC pragma form the same way because it does not implement it.
Rather than ask this record for a relaxed gate, the program suppresses that one diagnostic FROM INSIDE
with a diagnostic-control pragma both reference compilers implement, and restores the previous state
immediately afterwards. That is strictly better than a recorded deviation: the suppression is scoped
to a single line rather than to a translation unit, it is visible where it applies, and the audit gate
stays at full strength for this program as for every other -- which matters, because the gate is what
establishes the undefined-behaviour freedom that makes every divergence this suite reports meaningful.
Verified: the full gate, -Werror included, accepts this program with zero diagnostics under all four
reference drivers and under the alternate reference compiler.

THE FILE-NAME MACRO IS PATH-DEPENDENT AND IS NEVER PRINTED RAW. The harness copies each program into
its own per-cell workspace, so the real source path differs between cells and between targets, and
printing it would break byte-exact comparison in all twelve of them at once. Before the first line
directive the macro is therefore reduced to the relation "is not empty". Afterwards a line directive
supplies a virtual name written in this source, which is a compile-time constant rather than anything
the file system chose, so it is printed in full, byte for byte, alongside its length and its first and
its last character -- each of those three relations localising a different kind of corruption, a
truncation, a lost first byte or a lost terminator, to its own line. Printing the whole name is the
part that matters: an earlier form printed only the length and the two endpoints, and any misspelling
of the same length beginning with v and ending with c would have passed every oracle in all twelve
cells, leaving a defect in how the directive's string literal reaches the macro invisible. Verified by
building and running this source from two different absolute paths, one of them deeply nested, and once
more through a relative path: stdout was byte-identical every time, which is the proof that no path
reaches it. The recorded length of 14 is the exact character count of the virtual name written in the
directive, and it must be recounted if that name is ever changed. That name designates no file, is
never opened, and must never be created, so hermetic execution is preserved.

WHY THE DIAGNOSTIC-PREFIX EFFECT OF A LINE DIRECTIVE IS NOT ASSERTED. A line directive also changes
the file name and the line number a translator prints in front of its own diagnostics, and that is an
effect a reader might expect a preprocessor test to check. It is deliberately not checked, because
this suite compares stdout bytes and the raw wait status only and never compares standard error:
diagnostic wording legitimately differs between compilers, so comparing it would produce a flood of
divergences that say nothing about code correctness. The directive's effect is therefore observed
solely through the two source-position macros on stdout, which is where it is comparable at all.

WIDTH AND SIGNEDNESS NORMALIZATION, AND THE RESULT IT BUYS. Every printed value is an int or the
virtual name; no raw width, no plain-char value and no layout value is printed. The measured
consequence is what justifies this record's scope: all twelve cells -- four targets times three
optimization levels, each linked statically and executed natively on the host target or under that
target's emulator -- produced byte-identical stdout, exit status 0, and nothing on standard error.
That is why this record restricts no target, disables no oracle and needs no marker: after this
normalization there is no implementation-defined difference left for one of them to describe.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
line_delta=1
ignored_pragma_size_unchanged=1
ignored_pragma_align_unchanged=1
real_file_nonempty=1
line_after_directive=700
line_after_next=701
virtual_line=900
virtual_file=virtual_unit.c
virtual_file_len=14
virtual_file_first_is_v=1
virtual_file_last_is_c=1
END
